// THE COST IS THE REASON THIS FILE IS OFFLINE, AND IT IS THE LENS'S OWN SUBJECT MATTER. The
// corpus consumer re-ingests every subject through tokenize -> parse_production -> normalize in the
// interpreter, and the per-subject price is minutes rather than milliseconds against a corpus of
// thousands. The figure is not written here: census_for_paths below re-derives it on the host that
// asks, and population_for_root re-derives the denominator, which is what a reader needs rather
// than a digit that decays. That is not a scheduling inconvenience to be waited out; it is the same
// displaced cost DESIGN section 1 prices, and it is why the lens has stood roster-bound rather than
// corpus-bound since it was written.
//
// SO THIS FILE DECLARES NO WITNESS, AND THAT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. It was first
// written as claim/long/accumulator_copy_corpus_census_test.dag with two `test fn` rows and two
// exclusions -- a discovery row in gunbc.ci_layer_roots and a floor-preparation row -- and the
// floor refused both times, correctly. A CHANGED witness is admitted to the required floor
// regardless of any exclusion (cause=ChangedWitnessOutsidePreparedSubject, run 34018018622), which
// is exactly right: an exclusion that a new witness could opt into on the change that introduces it
// would be the escape hatch DESIGN section 5 forbids. A corpus-priced assertion therefore cannot be
// authored as a witness at all, and pretending otherwise buys a roster row that never runs. What
// lives here are entry points, run by a person, whose output belongs in a run.
//
// AND THE PRICE THAT DECIDES THIS IS NOT THE CORPUS -- IT IS ONE SUBJECT, WHICH IS A HARDER FACT
// THAN THE ONE THIS FILE FIRST RECORDED. The required floor's per-witness CPU line is 500ms
// (v2.workflow.required_floor required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms, an operator ruling whose
// two raises were repudiated). Interpreted ingest of a FIVE-LINE fixture measures about 3s, and a
// four-line String snippet with no filesystem read measures about the same: six times the line, at
// the smallest subject expressible. So no assertion in this lens that reaches the ingest can be
// enrolled at any subject size, which is why every witness this lens ever had is frozen or
// deferred -- a fact that was visible in the roster and had never been explained. Measured with
// claim_batch on this branch; run it rather than trusting this sentence.
//
// WHAT DOES EXECUTE ON EVERY PUSH IS THEREFORE NARROWER THAN THIS FILE ONCE CLAIMED, and the
// correction matters more than the claim did. It is the evidence that does not touch ingest: the
// coverage join in claim/complexity/accumulator_copy_corpus_path_test.dag, whose subject is the
// join and whose inputs are rows, and the typed-read pair in
// claim/complexity/accumulator_copy_unreadable_subject_test.dag, whose subject is the read arm.
// The ingest-priced predicates -- a planted copied accumulator caught through corpus_gate, a clean
// fold that stays clean, a readable subject reaching the lens -- pass by execution under
// claim_batch and are enrolled in nothing. That is the honest scope of this lane's claim.
//
// DISSOLUTION names the capability: ingest that does not re-parse source in the interpreter (native
// ingest, or a lens reading the compile graph). At that price the corpus form becomes affordable
// and these entry points become witnesses.
//
// ONE PROCESS CANNOT CARRY THE CORPUS, AND THE FAILURE IS A SEGMENTATION FAULT RATHER THAN A
// DIAGNOSTIC. Reproduced twice with census_for_paths over a stratified sample of the tracked
// corpus, which is the instrument to re-run rather than a batch size to copy from here: the run
// dies with SIGSEGV a few tens of subjects in, and the subject it dies ON analyses
// cleanly when handed to the same instrument alone -- so the cause is accumulation across subjects
// within a process, not any one subject's shape. That is worse than the cost: it means the
// whole-population form of corpus_gate is unreachable by construction and not merely slow, and the
// failure carries no cause, no locus and no count, so a sweep that dies looks exactly like a sweep
// that finished if only its exit status is read.
//
// So the census is run in BATCHES, which is why the instrument takes an explicit subject list
// beside the discovered-root form. The batch ceiling is an observation about this host and this
// build, not a modeled bound; it is stated here so the next author batches deliberately rather than
// discovering it as a silent truncation. Dissolution is the same trigger named above -- native
// ingest -- plus a driver that runs batches per process and joins their reports.
//
// THE WHOLE-CORPUS FORM IS ABSENT EVEN AS AN ENTRY POINT WITH A FIXED ANSWER. The obvious thing to
// write is a gate over the tracked corpus; nothing can run it, so it would be a documented red
// rather than an observed one -- roster membership standing in for a verdict. The subtree predicate
// below names a real discovered population small enough to execute, and the corpus form stays a run
// of census_for_root until its cost dissolves.
//
// THE NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER FOR THIS CLASS, AND WHAT IT MUST BE SUFFICIENT FOR. Enrolled in nothing is
// the honest standing for these entry points today, and it is also the standing that rots without a
// red ever appearing, so DESIGN section 4b's second meta-obligation applies: this is can-climb-
// after-one-grounding, not cannot-climb, and the grounding is named here rather than left as "when
// someone runs it".
//
// TRIGGER: an ingest realization for the accumulator-copy lens that does not re-parse source in the
// interpreter -- native ingest, or a lens that reads the compile graph the floor has already built.
//
// SUFFICIENT FOR -- AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETS THE BAR IS THE SMALLEST SUBJECT, NOT THE CORPUS.
// This is the finding to carry out of this lane and it belongs in the trigger rather than in a
// discussion: interpreted ingest of a FIVE-LINE fixture, and of a FOUR-LINE String with no
// filesystem read, both measure about six times the required floor's 500ms per-witness CPU line.
// At the smallest subject expressible. So this is not a budget that could be raised to fit the
// work; it is a floor that no ingest-reaching assertion can ever clear, and it retroactively
// explains why every witness this lens has ever had is frozen or deferred -- a fact that stood
// visible in the roster with no stated cause. A trigger that made large subjects affordable and
// left the smallest one at six times the line would restore nothing.
//
// Stated because a trigger naming less than the capability it restores is satisfied while the
// capability stays dead, and because the loss here is CORPUS-grain while the tempting trigger is
// per-subject: the capability is corpus_gate returning a verdict over the DISCOVERED
// population -- every module the module index yields, not a sample and not a subtree -- inside one
// required-lane budget and inside one process. Both halves are load-bearing and neither implies the
// other. A tenfold speedup per subject satisfies a per-subject reading of the trigger and still
// leaves the sweep hours long; and a sweep that fits the budget still dies with SIGSEGV partway
// through, a failure carrying no cause, no locus and no count, so it cannot be enrolled at any
// speed. That second half is filed as its own class --
// gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.sweep_terminated_by_process_death_rather_than_a_typed_refusal --
// because it outlives this lens: any single-process fold over a discovered population has it. An
// artifact that delivers only one half CONTRIBUTES to this trigger and does not retire it.
//
// UNTIL THEN the claim this lane makes is the cheap pair in
// claim/complexity/accumulator_copy_corpus_path_test.dag, and it is stated at that grain: the lens
// catches a planted copied accumulator and leaves a clean fold alone, over a population corpus_gate
// discovers. What is NOT claimed is that the tracked corpus is free of copied accumulators. Reading
// the absence of a red here as that verdict is the specific misreading this row exists to refuse.

// THE THREE INSTRUMENTS BELOW ARE ENTRY POINTS, NOT RESULTS. DESIGN section 6 asks that a
// measurement be cited by naming the producer that re-derives it. population_for_root answers how
// many subjects a root contributes; census_for_root and census_for_paths answer what the lens finds
// over a discovered root and over an explicit subject list respectively. Their output belongs in a
// run, never transcribed into this file.

// THE INGEST-PRICED PREDICATES, HOMED HERE WITH THE REST OF WHAT THE 500ms CPU LINE PRICES OUT.
// Each costs seconds of interpreted ingest per subject and so cannot be a witness; each is a real
// question with a real answer, run by a person or by the dissolution named above. a_readable_subject
// _still_reaches_the_lens is the deep positive control for the typed-read red in
// claim/complexity/accumulator_copy_unreadable_subject: that witness controls the READ arm, which
// is cheap and is the mechanism the red can fail open into; this one controls the whole path
// through to a clean verdict, and it is the half the budget does not admit.
fn a_readable_subject_still_reaches_the_lens() -> Bool {
  match file_gate_disposition(
    path: "test/fixture_roots/complexity_accumulator_copy/clean_linear.dag",
    refusal_ceiling: 0
  ) {
    GateClean => true
    GateNotEstablished { cause: _ } => false
    GateSuspectObserved { suspects: _ } => false
    GateRefusalBudgetExceeded { refusals: _, ceiling: _ } => false
  }
}

fn population_for_root(root: String) -> Int {
  length(xs: corpus_paths(pool_roots: [root]))
}

fn census_for_root(root: String) -> CorpusReport {
  corpus_report(pool_roots: [root])
}

fn census_for_paths(paths_csv: String) -> CorpusReport {
  corpus_report_from_rows(rows: corpus_rows_for_paths(paths: split(s: paths_csv, delimiter: ",")))
}

fn subtree_gate() -> CorpusGateDisposition {
  corpus_gate(pool_roots: ["dag/extdeps/filesystem"], population_floor: 5)
}

// A SUBTREE WHOSE SUBJECTS THE INGEST CANNOT READ IS NOT A CLEAN SUBTREE, and this witness says so
// rather than passing. dag/extdeps/filesystem is deliberately the subject: three of its six modules
// come back not-established under the v2 grammar, which is the reach fact the corpus consumer was
// built to surface. Asserting a no-suspect verdict here would have required either excluding the subtree or
// widening the disposition until unreadable counted as fine, and both are the coverage-by-illusion
// this file exists to end. The assertion is therefore that the gate REPORTS the unreadable
// population -- and its failure mode is the one that matters: if the ingest silently started
// answering clean for those subjects, this goes red.
fn corpus_subtree_gate_reports_its_unreadable_population() -> Bool {
  match subtree_gate() {
    CorpusNotEstablishedObserved { rows: _ } => true
    CorpusNoSuspectsObserved { files: _, refusal_sites: _, refusal_causes: _ } => false
    CorpusSuspectObserved { paths: _, sites: _ } => false
    CorpusPopulationBelowFloor { files: _, floor: _ } => false
    CorpusCoverageIncomplete { discovered: _, reported: _, missing: _ } => false
  }
}

// CAN THE IN-.DAG INGEST PATH FEED THE ASYMPTOTIC ENGINE OVER A REAL MODULE? ANSWERED BY EXECUTION:
// NO, AND THE FIRST READING OF THE EVIDENCE SAID YES. The instruments below are what settled it and
// they are kept because the answer is easy to get wrong in exactly the way it was got wrong here.
//
// ingested_root_shape answers TypeNode/Conj for a real module, and cost_lens over that same root
// answers Linear rather than refusing. Read together those two look like the asymptotic engine
// returning a verdict on a real module's cost. They are not. ingested_root_child_labels names the
// root's edges -- grammar_production_identity_node_projection and
// grammar_production_captured_node_projection -- so the tree is a GRAMMAR PRODUCTION TREE encoded
// in kernel nodes, and the Linear verdict is a cost of the parse shape, not of the program. That
// is why ingested_decl_cost_class answers decl-not-found: there are no declaration-named edges on
// the root for find_named_child to select, because normalize has not lowered anything.
//
// The consequence is the lane's shape. The copied-accumulator walker wants exactly this production
// tree -- it reads the grammar captures -- so it runs on real modules today. The budget gate's
// subjects are semantic Arrows, so feeding it a real module needs resolve / infer / fold_lowering,
// which is the compile-graph capability gunbc.plans.structural_quadratic_wall_coverage_audit names
// as the blocker. That blocker is real for the asymptotic path and is NOT a blocker for the shape
// detector; conflating the two is what makes the audit's stale prose read as if nothing can move.
//
// These are instruments, not witnesses -- run them and read the value; do not transcribe it back
// into this file.

fn ingested_root_shape(path: String) -> String {
  match ingest_tree(path: path) {
    SourceTreeNotEstablished { cause: _ } => "not-established"
    SourceTreeEstablished { root: r } =>
      match r.kind {
        TypeNode { connective: c } =>
          match c {
            Atom { identity: _ } => "TypeNode/Atom"
            Conj => "TypeNode/Conj"
            Disj => "TypeNode/Disj"
            Arrow => "TypeNode/Arrow"
            Cardinality => "TypeNode/Cardinality"
            Instantiation => "TypeNode/Instantiation"
          }
        ComputationNode { behavior: b } =>
          match b {
            Value => "ComputationNode/Value"
            Transform => "ComputationNode/Transform"
            Branch => "ComputationNode/Branch"
            Loop => "ComputationNode/Loop"
            Bind => "ComputationNode/Bind"
            Match => "ComputationNode/Match"
          }
      }
  }
}

fn ingested_cost_class(path: String) -> String {
  match ingest_tree(path: path) {
    SourceTreeNotEstablished { cause: _ } => "not-established"
    SourceTreeEstablished { root: r } =>
      match cost_lens(n: r) {
        Violates { diagnostic: _ } => "UnknownCost/refused"
        Holds { value: c } =>
          match asymptotic_class_of_cost(cost: c) {
            ClassConstant => "Constant"
            ClassLog => "Log"
            ClassLinear => "Linear"
            ClassLinearithmic => "Linearithmic"
            ClassPolynomial { degree: _ } => "Polynomial"
            ClassPolyLog { exponent: _ } => "PolyLog"
            ClassExponential => "Exponential"
            ClassFactorial => "Factorial"
            ClassUnknown => "Unknown"
          }
      }
  }
}

// THE ROSTER ROW'S OWN SHAPE, PROBED AGAINST A REAL MODULE. The budget gate's subject supply asks
// for an Arrow and takes its body child; a module root is a Conj of declarations, so the step this
// probe measures is whether one declaration of a real module can be selected and handed over in
// exactly that shape.
fn ingested_decl_cost_class(path: String, decl: String) -> String {
  match ingest_tree(path: path) {
    SourceTreeNotEstablished { cause: _ } => "not-established"
    SourceTreeEstablished { root: r } =>
      match find_named_child(root: r, name: symbol_intern_lexeme(lexeme: decl)) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "decl-not-found"
        Accepted { value: arrow, diagnostics: _ } =>
          match find_arrow_body_child(root: arrow) {
            Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "arrow-body-not-found"
            Accepted { value: body, diagnostics: _ } =>
              match cost_lens(n: body) {
                Violates { diagnostic: _ } => "UnknownCost/refused"
                Holds { value: c } =>
                  match asymptotic_class_of_cost(cost: c) {
                    ClassConstant => "Constant"
                    ClassLog => "Log"
                    ClassLinear => "Linear"
                    ClassLinearithmic => "Linearithmic"
                    ClassPolynomial { degree: _ } => "Polynomial"
                    ClassPolyLog { exponent: _ } => "PolyLog"
                    ClassExponential => "Exponential"
                    ClassFactorial => "Factorial"
                    ClassUnknown => "Unknown"
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn ingested_root_child_labels(path: String) -> String {
  match ingest_tree(path: path) {
    SourceTreeNotEstablished { cause: _ } => "not-established"
    SourceTreeEstablished { root: r } =>
      fold(r.children, init: "", f: fn(acc, e) {
        match e.label {
          Named { name: n } => join([acc, "|N:", symbol_lexeme(n)], "")
          _ => join([acc, "|P"], "")
        }
      })
  }
}

// THE ARM THAT USED TO ERASE ITS OWN CENSUS, WITNESSED ON A REAL SUBJECT. Review 61280 found that
// the no-suspect disposition dropped the refusal counts and causes the report had already computed.
// A witness that only checked the arm's NAME would have passed before the repair and after it, so
// this one asserts the payload: a positive refusal count and a non-empty cause census reaching a
// caller that reads the disposition alone.
//
// The subject is chosen because it is real and its residue is already known to the sibling roster,
// which budgets src/v2/lens/complexity_accumulator_copy/analyze.dag at a nonzero refusal ceiling --
// the accumulator-threading walk earns honest combiner_unregistered_carrier_reaches refusals from
// this very lens. So this is not a fixture arranged to have refusals; it is the lens reading its
// own analyzer and reporting what it cannot decide.
fn analyzer_disposition() -> CorpusGateDisposition {
  corpus_gate_disposition(
    report: corpus_report_from_rows(
      rows: corpus_rows_for_paths(
        paths: ["src/v2/lens/complexity_accumulator_copy/analyze.dag"]
      )
    ),
    population_floor: 1
  )
}

fn a_no_suspect_verdict_still_carries_its_refusal_census() -> Bool {
  match analyzer_disposition() {
    CorpusNoSuspectsObserved { files: _, refusal_sites: sites, refusal_causes: causes } =>
      (sites > 0) && (length(xs: causes) > 0)
    CorpusSuspectObserved { paths: _, sites: _ } => false
    CorpusNotEstablishedObserved { rows: _ } => false
    CorpusPopulationBelowFloor { files: _, floor: _ } => false
    CorpusCoverageIncomplete { discovered: _, reported: _, missing: _ } => false
  }
}
